            <details class="project-item" id="cloud-computing" name="projects">
                <summary class="project-header">
                    <img src="/project_images/cloud-computing-sdn-topology.png" alt="" loading="lazy" class="project-image">
                    <span class="project-header-text">
                        <span class="project-title">Cloud Computing Systems and Networks</span>
                        <span class="project-date">August 2026 – Present</span>
                        <span class="project-description">Adaptive OpenFlow routing, stateful firewall and NAT scaling, and distributed Rust MapReduce on Azure Kubernetes Service.</span>
                    </span>
                    <span class="project-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                </summary>
                <div class="project-expanded" id="cloud-computing-expanded">
                    <section>
                        <h3>Software-Defined Networking and Adaptive Routing</h3>
                        <p>An OS-Ken controller discovers network links, models them as a directed graph, and installs forwarding rules on Open vSwitch. It can select routes based on total latency, bottleneck capacity, or measured traffic. In Mininet, the shortest-path policy picked a 2 ms route with a 20 Mbps bottleneck. The widest-path policy picked an 80 Mbps route with 33 ms of inter-switch latency.</p>
                        <img src="/project_images/cloud-computing-sdn-topology.png" alt="Mininet topology with two low-delay 20 Mbps routes and an alternate 80 Mbps route with higher latency." loading="lazy">
                        <p>The adaptive controller estimates flow demand from sampled byte counters and subtracts that demand from the configured capacity of each link the flow traverses. One policy uses the remaining capacity to place new flows. The redistribution policy also reevaluates active flows periodically, assigns routes to the highest-volume flows first, and updates switch rules.</p>
                        <p>Under load, two flows offered a combined 85 Mbps on an 80 Mbps route. Moving the smaller flow onto a 20 Mbps alternative increased traffic delivery from <strong>93.14% to 97.11%</strong> during the measured interval. With a longer averaging window, the controller reacted later and delivered 90.42% under the same workload.</p>
                        <img src="/project_images/cloud-computing-routing-results.png" alt="Traffic delivery and per-flow bandwidth across the proactive controller and four redistribution settings." loading="lazy">
                        <p>I built parts of the Mininet topology and controller, tested the routing policies, and contributed fixes to bandwidth monitoring and redistribution.</p>

                        <h4>Technology Stack</h4>
                        <p><strong>Control plane</strong> (Python, OS-Ken, OpenFlow 1.3) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Network emulation</strong> (Mininet, Open vSwitch) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Validation</strong> (iperf, OpenFlow counters)</p>
                    </section>

                    <section>
                        <h3>Network Function Virtualization</h3>
                        <p>The control plane adds firewall and network address translation (NAT) capacity while keeping established TCP connections on their assigned instances. A REST API in the OS-Ken process registers service chains, starts Docker containers, and connects their interfaces to Open vSwitch before making them available to the controller. The deployment ran two chains on shared switches, one with a firewall followed by NAT and one with NAT alone.</p>
                        <p>The controller assigns each new TCP connection to instances in round-robin order and records that assignment for the lifetime of the flow. NAT can rewrite both an IP address and a TCP port. The controller records the translated reply tuple when traffic exits the NAT so return packets reach the same container and its Linux connection-tracking state. The switches use installed OpenFlow rules for subsequent packets.</p>
                        <p>All <strong>16 new TCP echo connections</strong> completed after scale-up, with traffic split across the original and added firewall and NAT instances on the first chain. In a separate test, two connections were already exchanging data when we scaled the chain. Both completed using their original instance assignments.</p>
                        <p>I set up the Docker and Open vSwitch topology, debugged forwarding logic, and ran the scale-up and connection-affinity tests.</p>

                        <h4>Technology Stack</h4>
                        <p><strong>Control plane</strong> (Python, OS-Ken, REST, OpenFlow 1.3) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Infrastructure</strong> (Docker, Open vSwitch, Linux iptables) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Validation</strong> (TCP echo, OpenFlow and interface counters)</p>
                    </section>

                    <section>
                        <h3>Distributed MapReduce on Azure</h3>
                        <p>A Rust scheduler dispatches map and reduce tasks to worker replicas on Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS). The scheduler and workers run as separate Kubernetes deployments and pull their images from Azure Container Registry. Workers register through etcd leases, and the scheduler watches for new workers and dispatches tasks over gRPC. Azure Blob Storage holds inputs, intermediate partitions, and final output. The same components can run locally with kind.</p>
                        <ol class="styled-list">
                            <li><strong>Map.</strong> The scheduler treats the blobs in an input container as one dataset and divides their bytes among map tasks. A map task can read ranges from multiple blobs. Workers run the submitted Python mapper, then hash each key to a reducer partition so equal keys meet at the same reducer.</li>
                            <li><strong>Shuffle and reduce.</strong> Each mapper writes one blob per partition. After mapping completes, each reducer fetches its partition from every mapper, sorts the records by key, runs the submitted Python reducer, and writes an output blob.</li>
                            <li><strong>Coordinate replicas.</strong> The replication design uses etcd for leader election and gRPC to replicate worker, job, and task state. A new follower receives a copy of the tables. On promotion, a follower resets in-progress work and attempts to rerun unfinished jobs.</li>
                        </ol>
                        <p>I validated the team’s AKS deployment with an end-to-end word-count job using three map tasks and two reduce tasks. I combined and sorted the reducer outputs and confirmed an exact match against the reference counts for that input.</p>

                        <h4>Technology Stack</h4>
                        <p><strong>Compute</strong> (Rust, Tokio, Python) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Infrastructure</strong> (Docker, Kubernetes, Azure Kubernetes Service, Azure Container Registry) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Coordination and storage</strong> (etcd, gRPC, Protocol Buffers, Azure Blob Storage)</p>
                    </section>
