            <details class="project-item" id="enoki" name="projects">
                <summary class="project-header">
                    <img src="/project_images/enoki-search.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" class="project-image">
                    <span class="project-header-text">
                        <span class="project-title">Enoki</span>
                        <span class="project-date">January 2026 – Present</span>
                        <span class="project-description">A research matchmaking platform that helps university corporate engagement teams find faculty for industry partnerships.</span>
                    </span>
                    <span class="project-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                </summary>
                <div class="project-expanded" id="enoki-expanded">
                    <h3>Project Overview</h3>
                    <p>My Georgia Tech CREATE-X team built Enoki for corporate engagement staff. A company may describe a problem in business terms, while faculty describe related work in the language of their fields. Staff can enter the original request, review a ranked list of researchers, and open their profiles and publications to decide whom to contact.</p>
                    <img src="/project_images/enoki-architecture.jpg" alt="Enoki system architecture. Faculty directories, DBLP, and Semantic Scholar feed a PostgreSQL index. Search expands the request, combines four retrieval rankings, and reranks faculty for the results view." loading="lazy">

                    <h3>Product Design</h3>
                    <p>Enoki starts with a natural-language search box. Staff can add a constraint without retyping the original request, and keyword tags show how the system interpreted it. Result cards give a relevance score and short, query-specific reasons for each match. Opening a profile reveals the researcher’s background, research summary, publication links, and contact information. A profile-specific chat assistant can look up that researcher’s indexed papers to answer follow-up questions.</p>
                    <img src="/project_images/enoki-search.jpg" alt="Enoki results for robust autonomous vehicles, showing relevance scores and brief explanations for the top faculty matches." loading="lazy">

                    <h3>Faculty Data and Indexing</h3>
                    <p>The ingestion pipeline starts with Georgia Tech’s College of Computing directory, uses DBLP to find author name variants, and resolves those names to Semantic Scholar author records and publications. It saves the directory profile first so a failed publication lookup can be retried without losing the faculty record.</p>
                    <p>The pipeline can run scraping, summarization, and embedding generation independently. It summarizes papers and faculty research, generates 1,536-dimensional OpenAI embeddings in batches, and refreshes the full-text search fields after embedding. PostgreSQL stores faculty records, publications, and authorship links alongside their embeddings. GIN indexes support full-text queries, and pgvector computes cosine similarity in the same database.</p>

                    <h3>Hybrid Search and Reranking</h3>
                    <ol class="styled-list">
                        <li><strong>Expand the request.</strong> GPT-4o mini translates the industry brief into academic terminology. The search uses concise terms for keyword matching and embeds the expanded text for semantic matching.</li>
                        <li><strong>Retrieve from four rankings.</strong> PostgreSQL runs full-text and vector searches over both faculty profiles and individual papers. Keyword results use PostgreSQL’s <code>ts_rank_cd</code> ranking. The system maps paper hits back to their authors and uses reciprocal rank fusion to combine the lists, so a relevant paper can surface a researcher whose profile doesn’t contain the same terms.</li>
                        <li><strong>Rerank a shortlist.</strong> The API fetches candidate profiles and prioritizes papers that drove the retrieval match. GPT-4o then scores the shortlisted researchers in parallel and returns plain-language match reasons. Keeping model scoring to the shortlist limits calls per query.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <h3>AWS Infrastructure and Deployment</h3>
                    <ul class="styled-list">
                        <li><strong>Web and API.</strong> CloudFront serves the React build from S3, with an <code>index.html</code> fallback for client-side routes. An HTTPS Application Load Balancer routes API requests to Dockerized FastAPI tasks on ECS Fargate and runs health checks during deployments.</li>
                        <li><strong>Storage and access.</strong> Amazon RDS runs PostgreSQL 16 with pgvector. The database is configured without public access and with automated backups. FastAPI validates signed tokens and selects the university’s PostgreSQL schema for research queries. Account and tenant records live in shared tables.</li>
                        <li><strong>Operations.</strong> AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store supplies credentials to the API tasks, and CloudWatch collects logs. Tenant setup and ingestion run as one-off Fargate tasks independently of the API service. A validation script checks for gaps in publications, summaries, and embeddings.</li>
                        <li><strong>Delivery.</strong> AWS CDK defines separate frontend and backend CloudFormation stacks. GitHub Actions builds and ships the frontend to S3, then invalidates the CloudFront cache. The backend workflow pushes a Git-SHA-tagged image to ECR and updates the ECS service. The ECS deployment circuit breaker enables automatic rollback when a rollout fails to stabilize.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>My Contribution</h3>
                    <p>I prototyped saved searches across the React interface, FastAPI endpoints, and PostgreSQL schema. Each bookmark saves the original query, its refinement context, and a JSONB snapshot of the ranked results. Reopening a bookmark restores those matches without calling the models again. Older bookmarks without snapshots can still rerun the original search.</p>

                    <h3>Pilot Evaluation</h3>
                    <p>Georgia Tech Corporate Engagement staff tested Enoki with real industry requests and reported that it surfaced promising faculty they had missed in their initial search. The team also checked expected matches on known queries such as “drone SLAM.” The pilot identified model latency as a bottleneck for staff who want to refine a search several times in one sitting.</p>

                    <h3>Technology Stack</h3>
                    <p><strong>Frontend</strong> (React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Backend</strong> (Python, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Data and search</strong> (PostgreSQL 16, pgvector, GIN full-text indexes) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>AI</strong> (GPT-4o, GPT-4o mini, text-embedding-3-small) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Infrastructure</strong> (Docker, AWS CDK, CloudFront, S3, ECS Fargate, ECR, RDS, CloudWatch) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>CI/CD</strong> (GitHub Actions)</p>
