            <details class="project-item" id="led-brightness" name="projects">
                <summary class="project-header">
                    <img src="/project_images/led-brightness-cover.png" alt="" loading="lazy" class="project-image">
                    <span class="project-header-text">
                        <span class="project-title">LED Brightness Peripheral</span>
                        <span class="project-date">January 2025 – May 2025</span>
                        <span class="project-description">A VHDL peripheral that lets SCOMP programs dim and animate ten LEDs on the DE10 FPGA board.</span>
                    </span>
                    <span class="project-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                </summary>
                <div class="project-expanded" id="led-brightness-expanded">
                    <h3>Project Overview</h3>
                    <p>I built an FPGA peripheral that lets a SCOMP assembly program set which LEDs light up, how bright they appear, and how they animate in a single write.</p>

                    <h3>SCOMP Programming Interface</h3>
                    <p>An <code>OUT</code> instruction to I/O address <code>0x20</code> sends the peripheral a 16-bit control word. Bits 15–12 set a shared brightness in steps of 10 percentage points from 0% to 100%, bits 11–10 select the display mode, and bits 9–0 specify the LED pattern with one bit per LED.</p>
                    <img src="/project_images/led-brightness-control-word.png" alt="Example control word with brightness level 0111, mode 11, and LED pattern 0010010100." loading="lazy">

                    <h3>Display Modes</h3>
                    <ul class="styled-list">
                        <li><strong>Static</strong> (<code>00</code>) holds the selected pattern at the requested brightness.</li>
                        <li><strong>Breathing</strong> (<code>01</code>) fades the selected LEDs up to the requested brightness and back to off.</li>
                        <li><strong>Barrel Shift</strong> (<code>10</code>) rotates the pattern left, wrapping the leftmost bit around to the right.</li>
                        <li><strong>Pattern Inverse</strong> (<code>11</code>) flips the LED selection so the LEDs outside the original pattern light up.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3>Technical Implementation</h3>
                    <p>The VHDL controller uses a gamma-correction lookup table to turn each brightness setting into an 8-bit pulse-width modulation (PWM) threshold. The correction makes the brightness steps appear more even to the eye. A counter sets how long the LEDs stay on in each PWM cycle, and the current pattern determines which LEDs receive that pulse.</p>

                    <h3>Test Program</h3>
                    <p>The SCASM test program cycles through all four modes and all eleven brightness settings. It reads the board’s slide switches to select the LED pattern and shows the current brightness level on the seven-segment display.</p>

                    <h3>Technology Stack</h3>
                    <p><strong>Hardware</strong> (DE10 board with Cyclone V FPGA) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Hardware design</strong> (VHDL) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Test program</strong> (SCOMP assembly with SCASM) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>FPGA tools</strong> (Quartus Prime Lite and ModelSim) &nbsp;|&nbsp; <strong>Lookup table generation</strong> (Python)</p>
